f) 1-sqrt(3)<0, hence the power <1.
g) 2^x is <1, =1, >1 respectively for x<0, x=0, x>0.
h) 2^(1-x) is >1, =1, <1 respectively for x<1, x=1, x>1.
Equality cases and variable-dependent alternatives are part of the requested comparisons.

### 684
a) (1,+infinity).
b) (0,1).
c) (2,32).
d) [1/2,2].
e) [1,2].
Strictly increasing continuous 2^x maps finite endpoints to their powers, preserving endpoint inclusion; it tends to zero as x tends to -infinity but never attains zero.

