### 717
No, not in general. Use factors 1.2 and 1.3: product 1.56 has fractional part 0.56, whereas 0.2*0.3=0.06. Thus the proposed general identity is false.
The integer parts generate additional cross terms; dropping them before multiplication is invalid.

### 718
No, not always. 0.6+0.6=1.2 has integer part 1, while floor(0.6)+floor(0.6)=0.
In general floor(s+t)=floor(s)+floor(t)+floor({s}+{t}); equality occurs exactly when {s}+{t}<1. The correction is 1 when the fractional parts sum to at least 1.

