c) floor(x) is always an integer, so {floor(x)}=0. Draw the entire x axis y=0.
All three graphs have domain R; b and c are continuous despite the inner floor/fractional-part operations.

### 720
a) floor(x)=0 means 0<=x<1 with y arbitrary: the infinite vertical strip [0,1) x R. Draw x=0 solid/included and x=1 dashed/excluded.
b) floor(y)=1 means 1<=y<2 with x arbitrary: R x [1,2). Draw y=1 solid/included and y=2 dashed/excluded.
c) The system is [1,2) x [2,3). Shade that rectangle; its left and bottom boundaries are included, its right and top excluded. The corner (1,2) is included, the other three integer corners are not part of this rectangle.
No restriction is added to the unconstrained coordinate in a or b.

### 721
Equation floor(x)=floor(y) is the union over all integers n of the half-open squares [n,n+1) x [n,n+1).
Draw diagonal-aligned unit squares, for example [-1,0)^2, [0,1)^2, [1,2)^2, continuing in both directions. In each square left/bottom are included and top/right excluded. The integer diagonal point (n+1,n+1) belongs to the next square, not the preceding square; mixed corners (n+1,n) and (n,n+1) are excluded.
This is a two-dimensional set, not merely the line y=x and not the entire band |x-y|<1.

### 722
a) 10^(a+b).
b) 10^(3a).
c) 10^(b/2).
d) 10^((2/3)a+b/5).
Use 31.5=10^a and 0.42=10^b exactly; positive values justify taking real fractional powers.

### 723
Method: use the observed source y=10^x graph on 0<=x<=1 to read inverse exponents of normalized values between 1 and 10. Rescale by powers of 10, apply exponent arithmetic, then read the forward value from the same graph. Coarse graphical estimates below are explicitly NOT calculator values.
a) Inverse graph read for 1.95 is about 0.29. Divide by 5 to about 0.058; the forward graph near that abscissa gives about 1.14.
b) Write 23=10*2.3. Inverse read for 2.3 is about 0.36, giving exponent about 1.36. Multiply by 5/6 to about 1.13; forward graph near 0.13, then multiply ordinate by 10, gives about 13.6.
c) Write 0.0041=10^-3*4.1. Inverse read for 4.1 is about 0.61; exponent about -2.39. Multiply by 2/3 to about -1.59=-2+0.41. Forward graph near 0.41 gives about 2.6, then multiply by 0.01: about 0.026 (a slightly finer hand read about 0.0255-0.026).
d) Write 0.18=10^-1*1.8 and 400=10^2*4. Inverse reads for 1.8 and 4 are about 0.26 and 0.60. Combined exponent is about 0.2*(-1+0.26)+(2+0.60)/3, about 0.72; forward graph gives about 5.2.
The graph has rough strokes and finite grid resolution. None of these readings warrants four or six significant digits. The exact mathematical expressions and separately labeled high-precision calculations are included in the analytic appendix solely for independent numeric checking.

